@page "/help"

<PageTitle>Help</PageTitle>

<h1>Help</h1>
<p class="lead">Tickle TV turns your video files into a TV channel that runs all day, with commercial breaks, just like real TV. This page explains each step in plain words.</p>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Step 1: Scan your videos</h2>
    <p>Scanning tells Tickle TV where your files are. It reads each file once to learn how long it is and whether it has chapter marks, then sorts it by its name, or by the details in a <em>.nfo</em> file next to it when there is one.</p>
    <p>Open the <a href="scan">Scan</a> page. Add each folder that holds videos (subfolders are included), then press <strong>Scan all my folders</strong>. A progress bar shows how far along it is, and you can keep using the app meanwhile. Scanning a big library takes a while, about ten files per second. Scan again any time you add files; only new or changed files are read.</p>
    <h3>How files get sorted</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Details files (.nfo):</strong> if you use Kodi, Jellyfin, Emby, tinyMediaManager or a similar program, it leaves a small <em>.nfo</em> file next to each video, and a <em>tvshow.nfo</em> in the show's folder. Tickle TV reads those first: show name, season and episode number, episode title, air date and genre. They beat anything guessed from the file name, and rescanning picks up any <em>.nfo</em> file that has changed. Your own answers on the Needs attention page still win over both.</li>
        <li><strong>TV episodes</strong> without a details file are recognised by a season and episode number in the name, such as <em>S04E04</em> or <em>3x07</em>. The show name comes from the file name, or from the folder above the "Season" folder.</li>
        <li><strong>Commercials</strong> are recognised by words like <em>Commercial</em>, <em>Ad</em>, <em>PSA</em>, or <em>Promo</em> in the file name or folder name. A folder called <em>Commercials</em> makes everything inside it a commercial.</li>
        <li><strong>Bumpers</strong> (station IDs, "we'll be right back" cards) are recognised by <em>Bumper</em>, <em>Ident</em>, or <em>Station ID</em> in the name or folder.</li>
        <li><strong>Filler</strong> is a short video that plays when a commercial break has a few seconds left over. Name it with <em>Filler</em> or <em>Fallback</em>.</li>
        <li>Folders named after a decade, like <em>80s</em> or <em>90s</em>, and folders under them, like <em>Cars</em> or <em>Food</em>, become labels on everything inside.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Step 2: Label what the scan couldn't</h2>
    <p>Go to <a href="review">Needs attention</a>. Each file there has a short note saying why it's listed. For each one:</p>
    <ol class="steps">
        <li>Click <strong>Watch</strong>. The file opens in your usual video player so you can see what it is.</li>
        <li>Pick what it is from the list: a commercial, a TV episode, a bumper, and so on.</li>
        <li>Fill in what you know. For a commercial, the brand and a category. For an episode, the show, season, and episode number.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Save</strong>. The file leaves the list, and your answer is kept even if you scan again.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If you truly can't tell what something is, choose <strong>Not something to air</strong> and it will never be scheduled.</p>
    <h3>Labeling many files at once</h3>
    <p>When several files need the same answer, tick the box beside each one (or press <strong>Select all shown</strong>). A panel appears at the top. Fill in only what they share, such as "these are all TV episodes of Modern Marvels, season 0", and press <strong>Apply</strong>. Anything you leave blank keeps each file's own value. For episodes, "Number them in order" gives the ticked files episode numbers in the order shown, starting at the number you type.</p>
    <h3>The filters at the top</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Don't know what it is:</strong> the name and folder gave no clue what kind of file this is.</li>
        <li><strong>Episode details missing:</strong> a TV episode with no show, season, or episode number.</li>
        <li><strong>Bumpers I couldn't sort:</strong> a file from a bumper folder that isn't marked as a station ID, a break card, or a show promo. Only station IDs and break cards are used around commercial breaks.</li>
        <li><strong>Commercials without a category</strong> and <strong>without a brand:</strong> commercials missing those labels.</li>
        <li><strong>Episodes without chapter marks:</strong> these play straight through with no commercial breaks inside them, then get a short block of commercials at the end so the next show starts on a 5-minute mark. This is informational and is not included in "All".</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Browsing and fixing anything: the Library page</h2>
    <p>The <a href="library">Library</a> page shows every file Tickle TV knows about. Type part of a name to search, or narrow by kind, show, category, or label. Each file has the same <strong>Watch</strong>, <strong>Show in folder</strong>, and <strong>Edit</strong> buttons as the Needs attention page, and you can tick several files to label them together. Use it to fix a wrong guess, move an episode to the right show, or re-categorise a batch of commercials. Labels shown in blue are ones a person set; those always win over the scan's guesses.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Channels: more than one station, and picture settings</h2>
    <p>The <a href="channels">Channels</a> page lists every channel ErsatzTV knows about. You can add a channel with a few plain choices: picture quality (480p, 720p, or 1080p), video format, whether to use the graphics card to encode, whether to pass through files that already match, and whether to even out the volume. Tickle TV writes ErsatzTV's channel files for you and gives the new channel a starter schedule. <strong>ErsatzTV only reads channel settings when it starts, so restart it after adding or changing a channel.</strong> Each channel has its own schedule on the Schedule page.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Step 3: Set the daily schedule</h2>
    <p>Open <a href="schedule">Schedule</a>. Each row is a time slot: a start time and a show. From that time until the next slot starts, the channel plays that show's episodes back to back, for example <em>cartoons from 6am, Star Trek from 8pm</em>. The last slot runs overnight until the first one. For each slot you can choose:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Episode order:</strong> in order by air date, or shuffled so every episode airs once before any repeats.</li>
        <li><strong>Several shows in one slot:</strong> press <em>add another show to this slot</em> to make a block, such as three sitcoms sharing the evening. The shows take turns, one episode each. Choose whether they go in the order you listed them or at random (a random pick never plays the same show twice in a row).</li>
        <li><strong>Each show ends on:</strong> the half hour, the hour, or the next 5 or 10 minutes. Commercials fill the time in between.</li>
        <li><strong>Breaks per show:</strong> how many commercial breaks to put inside an episode that has chapter marks.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Channel settings below the grid cover the defaults: how long before a commercial can repeat, where bumpers go, and whether slots always start on time. Press <strong>Save schedule</strong> when you're done.</p>
    <h3>Holiday episodes</h3>
    <p>The scan marks holiday episodes, commercials and bumpers for you: a title or description with words like <em>Christmas</em>, <em>Xmas</em>, <em>Treehouse of Horror</em>, <em>Thanksgiving</em> or <em>Valentine</em>, or an episode that first aired in the ten days leading up to a holiday. They show as labels such as <em>holiday:christmas</em> in the Library, and the My shows page counts them per show. Guesses wait under <strong>Holiday episodes to confirm</strong> on the Needs attention page, each with the reason and a <em>Yes</em> or <em>No</em> button; you can also press <strong>Edit</strong> on any file and answer <em>Holiday episode?</em>. Your answer sticks. Christmas Eve episodes count as Christmas.</p>
    <p>Then, on the Schedule page, any slot can say <em>Holiday episodes first, then the rest</em> or <em>Holiday episodes only</em> and name the holiday. "First" plays each holiday episode once (in the slot's order) and then carries on with regular episodes; it starts afresh every year. "Only" plays nothing but holiday episodes, round and round. Holiday picks keep their own place in line, so the everyday rotation is not disturbed. The page tells you how many holiday episodes each show in the slot has.</p>
    <h3>Different days, different grids</h3>
    <p>A channel can have more than one grid, called a <strong>day plan</strong>. You might have Normal Day, Weekend, and Christmas. On the Schedule page, type a name next to <em>New plan called</em> and press <strong>Make a copy of this plan</strong>; then change the copy's slots. The <a href="calendar">Calendar</a> page says which dates use which plan, with rules in plain words: Saturdays and Sundays use Weekend; the week leading up to Christmas Day uses Christmas. Common holidays are built in, including ones that move like Easter and Thanksgiving, and you can add your own date ranges. You can also tick days on the calendar and pick a plan for just those days. A plan takes over at its first time slot, so the night before finishes as usual.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Step 4: Build the schedule</h2>
    <p>Tickle TV fills each slot with episodes, adds commercial breaks between scenes, and pads each show so the next one starts on time.</p>
    <p>Open the <a href="build">Build</a> page. <strong>Preview</strong> shows the next 24 hours as a list of programmes without changing anything, along with anything worth a look, such as a show that runs past the next slot. <strong>Build and send to ErsatzTV</strong> writes it for real. Build once a day, or whenever you change the schedule. It joins in at the end of whatever is playing, so the show on air is never interrupted.</p>
</div>

<div class="card">
    <h2>Words you'll see</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
        <dt>Bumper</dt><dd>A few seconds of video like a station logo or "we'll be right back". One plays just before a show starts and one just after it ends.</dd>
        <dt>Filler</dt><dd>A video that loops to cover the last few seconds of a break when no commercial fits exactly.</dd>
        <dt>Chapter marks</dt><dd>Points inside an episode file that mark scene changes. Tickle TV puts commercial breaks there, so they land between scenes.</dd>
        <dt>Padding</dt><dd>Extra commercials added so a show ends exactly on the hour or half hour.</dd>
        <dt>Catalog</dt><dd>Tickle TV's list of everything it has scanned, plus the labels you've added.</dd>
    </dl>
</div>
